{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"uAYKZws_DGpy"},"source":[" <H1 style=\"color:navy;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"><B><I><Center>FRUIT SHELF-LIFE DETECTION</Center></I></B></p></H1>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"5C2OPj4VPor9"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Group 6: assessment_10_05_2023</H3>\n","<body><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">IFEOLUWA OGUNTIMEHIN, s5532335@bournemouth.ac.uk</p>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">ADESOLA OKETOLA, s5503335@bournemouth.ac.uk</p>    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">TOBI GBADEBO-OGUNMEFUN, s5519065@bournemouth.ac.uk</p>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">AMAL SABO, s5439289@bournemouth.ac.uk</p></body>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"ZB9FtDBTPor-"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Abstract: -</H3>\n","<body>    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> A crucial application in the fields of agriculture and food processing is fruit shell identification. Fruit shell detection has a big impact on the safety and quality control of fruits and vegetables throughout harvest, transportation, and processing. The overview of fruit shell detection in this study includes the various methods and tools that are currently employed. the identification of fruit shells using machine learning methods and image processing techniques. Additionally, it emphasises the significance of precise fruit shell identification and how it affects food safety and quality assurance. The report also discusses difficulties and potential prospects for fruit shell detection. The article offers a thorough review of fruit shell detection and prospective uses for it in the food processing sector.</p>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"irdGzJTBPor_"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Introduction</H3>\n","<body>    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> Finding fruit shells is an important job in the fields of agriculture and food production. During harvest, transportation, and processing, fruit and vegetable quality control and safety are greatly impacted by the detection of fruit shells. To lower the danger of infection and raise the general quality of fruits and vegetables, it is crucial to identify shells on produce and remove them. Fruit shell identification has been made possible by a number of methods and tools, including as image processing, machine learning, and computer vision.</p>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> These methods have made it possible to create fruit shell detection systems that are reliable and effective for a variety of uses in the food processing sector. This work intends to present an overview of fruit shell identification in this context and to highlight the various methods and tools that are currently employed. The significance of precise fruit shell detection and its effects on food safety and quality assurance will also be covered. Additionally, the difficulties and prospects for fruit shell detection will be discussed.</p>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"T-XHTi81PosA"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Literature Survey: -</H3>\n","<body>    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">One method for determining the fruit's shelf life relies on chemical sensors. These sensors are able to identify the volatile organic compounds (VOCs) that fruits produce as they mature, which may be used to calculate how long fruit will keep after being picked. For instance, Loutfi et al. (2013) discovered that a metal oxide sensor array can discriminate between various fruit ripening phases by detecting VOCs from peaches and nectarines. A colorimetric sensor array was used in a separate investigation by Wang et al. (2018) to identify the VOCs of kiwifruit at various ripening stages.</p>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Another well-liked method for determining fruit shelf-life is optical sensors. These sensors analyse the chemical makeup of fruits and establish the phases of ripening using visible and near-infrared (NIR) spectroscopy. As an illustration, Zhu et al. (2018) suggested using a hyperspectral imaging system to assess the peach's interior quality and freshness. According to the study, the method has an accuracy of over 90% for identifying the various stages of peach ripeness. Additionally, Zhang et al. (2018) accurately predicted the soluble solids content (SSC) of blueberries with an R2 value of 0.94 using NIR spectroscopy to analyse the SSC of blueberries.</p>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">The detection of fruit shelf-life also makes extensive use of machine learning methods. These algorithms may examine the information gathered by chemical and optical sensors and create models to forecast the fruit's shelf-life. For instance, based on the VOCs and colour characteristics of the fruits, Xu et al. (2019) used a support vector machine (SVM) method to forecast the shelf-life of strawberries. The study's prediction of the strawberry's shelf life was 85% accurate. Based on hyperspectral imaging data, Zhang et al. (2019) created a deep learning method to forecast the ripening phases of kiwifruit.</p>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">A detection method for typical tomato fruit physiological diseases based on the convolutional neural network YOLOv2 was proposed by Zhao, J. Datasets were pretreated using a number of techniques to lower the possibility of overfitting and boost detection precision. An improved k-means clustering approach was used to enhance network discovery. The recommended detection method worked effectively for tomato fruits that were both healthy and those that had common physiological abnormalities. The mAP (mean Average Precision) for the YOLOv2 tomato fruit detection network was 97.24 percent. Additionally, comparative experiments showed that training the network using RGB pictures was successful.</p>\n","</body>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"DZhGztciPosB"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Problem Statement: -</H3>\n","<body>    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Oftentimes when we walk past the ‘fruits and vegetables and/or foods’ aisles in grocery stores or supermarkets, we are repelled by the pungent smells. This occurs because of a few reasons, such as those items have reached their time of minimum durability (or shelf life), or inadequate packaging, or simple expiry beyond being edible. Typically, those grocery stores and supermarkets are often guided by the expiry dates written on the products by the manufacturer to stock on items; oftentimes the ‘smell’ or ‘appearance’ of those items is irrelevant to those stores. Consumers end up purchasing items with reduced nutritional value simply because there has been a loss of freshness either shortly after the harvest or while in the grocery stores</p>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Practically, the freshness of perishable food items, particularly fruits and vegetables, is not just determined by the ‘expiry date’ printed on the packaging; there are other factors which come to play, such as sensory human functions of smell (olfactory), sight or taste (gustatory) as suggested by (Karamchandani, Sekhani, Nair, & Shah, 24-26 September 2021). However, very rarely are consumers allowed to inspect perishable food items in grocery stores by smelling or tasting them because they are sealed in packages, and so consumers are left with the option to inspect by ‘looking’ and to use the expiry dates on such items. Left with the choice of ‘inspection by sight’, we propose a convolution neural network to predict the shelf life of some perishable fruits such as apples, bananas, strawberries, avocados and mangoes (also known as climacteric fruits). A total of 5 fruits were chosen, makings a multiclass classification problem.\n","</p>\n","</body>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"f2jTIT3_PosC"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Aim</H3>\n","<body>    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">This project aims to develop a machine learning algorithm which can predict or label if a fruit is unripe (OK for later consumption), ripe (Ok for immediate\n","consumption) or overripe/rotten (not OK for consumption at all). Ideally, we may have a mixture of unripe, ripe and/or overripe/rotten fruits in one\n","photo. We want to see if the machine learning algorithm can correctly identify/label each of them according to their freshness.</p>\n","<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Objectives</H3>\n","<body>    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> The following objectives have been identified:\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"><li>Web scrape images of the five fruits in the three classifications to create a dataset to train our model.</li>\n","<li>Develop a model that can inspect the fruit images in real time and categorize it with low latency.</li>\n","<li>Make a comparison of the models' performances based on accuracy, precision, recall, etc.</li>\n","<li>Designing the model to be scalable so that it can be easily deployed in different settings, from small-scale fruit processing facilities to large-scale fruit distribution centres</li>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"IrVLhL78PosD"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Methodology</H3>\n","<body>    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> The following steps make up the approach for the Fruit Shelf-Life Detection project:\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"><li>Data collection: The first phase is acquiring photographs of certain fruits in the three stages of ripeness—unripe, ripe, and overripe. Fruits of various sizes, shapes, and textures will be included in the dataset, which will be compiled by web scraping the internet using the beautiful-soup package.</li>\n","<li>Data preparation: After the dataset has been gathered, the preprocessed fruit photos will be used to enhance the model's suitability for training. Resizing, colour correction, normalisation, picture filtering, edge detection, contour detection, and other processes will be required. The OpenCV library will be used to carry out all of these activities.</li>\n","<li>The following stage is to extract and discover the intriguing elements in the preprocessed fruit photos that may be used to enhance the model's performance. Shape analysis, texture analysis, and colour histogram will all be included in the characteristics.</li>\n","<li>Model training: Using the retrieved features and matching fruit labels (unripe, ripe, and overripe), we will train our machine learning model after the features have been extracted. We will expose our dataset to a variety of models, including Random Forest, Convolutional Neural Networks, and Support Vector Machine classifiers.</li>\n","<li>Evaluation of models: Various metrics, including accuracy, mean squared error, precision, F1 score, etc., will be calculated to assess each model's performance. In order to evaluate its performance, we will also plot the RUC and AUC curves or other quality measures.</li></ol>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">The aforementioned technique will be applied step-by-step to make sure that each phase is finished before going on to the next. By the time the process is complete, we hope to have a model that can precisely identify the fruit's shelf life based on its photos."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"6GUzTvubVZq3"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Installation and Importation of Dependencies</H3>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"ZQ5W1q3Ibox9"},"source":["<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"><li>NumPy is a Python library for scientific computing that allows for numerical calculation. It has utilities for integrating C/C++ and Fortran code, a robu