ssign them to an individual variable\n","im1t, im2t, im3t = map(cv2.imread, apple_test)\n","im4t, im5t, im6t = map(cv2.imread, bananas_test)\n","im7t, im8t, im9t = map(cv2.imread, mangoes_test)\n","im10t, im11t, im12t = map(cv2.imread, avocados_test)\n","im13t, im14t, im15t = map(cv2.imread, strawberries_test)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"PVMIIOwvYgwz"},"source":["Shown below are few images from the our Capture images we will be using test the accuracy our Neural Networks"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":675},"id":"c1cA2zkmUGan","outputId":"f4be66d3-fddc-473f-8544-ac764b93f76e"},"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x800 with 20 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["# Display the test images\n","ims = [im1t, im2t, im3t, im4t, im5t, im6t, im7t, im8t, im9t, im10t, im11t, im12t, im13t, im14t, im15t]\n","show_imgs(ims, categories, fruits);"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"qldyfrLaiLAh"},"source":["# Image Preprocessing"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"ZYJaQqvAgTfk"},"source":["From the images displayed above, it can be noticed that our images are of diferent sizes.\n","According to (Pulkit Sharma, 2019)(https://www.analyticsvidhya.com/blog/2019/12/image-augmentation-deep-learning-pytorch/) in his article wrote about guidelines to follow to preprocess images so they can best fit the model and generate accurate result. These includes:\n","*   Making sure the size of all images classes are similar\n","*   Applying image augmentation when we have relatively less number of data samples. Which in our project we have just 35 sample per class.\n","\n","From an article written by (Suki Lau, 2017)(https://towardsdatascience.com/image-augmentation-for-deep-learning-histogram-equalization-a71387f609b2) about image augmentation, she said deep neural networks require a substantial amount of training data to achieve high performance. In order to create a robust image classifier with limited training data, image augmentation is often employed to enhance the performance of deep networks. Image augmentation generates additional training images by applying various processing techniques or combinations of multiple methods, including random rotation, shifts, shearing, and flips, among others. This artificial expansion of the training dataset helps improve the model's ability to generalize and adapt to new input data.\n","\n","It is important that our images are normalized. This was discussed thoroughly during the Lectures. This preprocessing technique scales the pixel values of the images to a specific range, usually [0, 1] for Min-max normalization or [-1, 1] for Mean normalization also known as standardization. It makes the training process more efficient, stable, and faster by providing consistent input data to the neural network. (Jason Brownlee, 2019)(https://machinelearningmastery.com/how-to-improve-neural-network-stability-and-modeling-performance-with-data-scaling/) also stated in his article that normalization can also help in reducing the impact of illumination differences and improving the convergence of the training process.\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"RnDm7ZWxxnZx"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Load images and convert them to PyTorch tensors\n","# Set the path to your image directory\n","image_dir = \"/content/images\""]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"RR2L0pVD4-ZP"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Load image and convert to tensors\n","dataset = ImageFolder(root=image_dir, transform=transforms.ToTensor())"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"yNPvJFvMZv_e"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from (https://discuss.pytorch.org/t/computing-the-mean-and-std-of-dataset/34949#:~:text=import%20torch%0Afrom,0.3749%2C%200.3992%2C%200.4505%5D))\n","# Create a function to determine the mean and standard deviation of the image dataset\n","def mean_std(data):\n","    # Calculate the mean and standard deviation for each channel\n","    μ = 0.0                                # mu (μ) represents mean\n","    σ = 0.0                                # sigma (σ) represents Standard Deviation\n","    for image, _ in data:\n","        μ += image.mean([1, 2])\n","        σ += image.std([1, 2])\n","\n","    μ /= len(data)\n","    σ /= len(data)\n","\n","    return μ, σ"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"1iqgSw9Xppxz","outputId":"2c89f67d-a29d-4af8-fe0b-48cd1e900cc2"},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["The values of the mean and standard deviation of our dataset: (tensor([0.6305, 0.5799, 0.4432]), tensor([0.2244, 0.2391, 0.2540]))\n"]}],"source":["dataset_stats = mean_std(dataset)\n","print(f\"The values of the mean and standard deviation of our dataset: {dataset_stats}\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"V3C2Y3ZFZkPi"},"source":["<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> We have been able to determine the mean and standard deviation values our dataset.\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> Next we will be applying transformation which includes:\n","*   Image Resizing (image height = 150 and image width = 150)\n","*   Augementation of the images. Theses includes: Horizontal Flip, Rotation and affine\n","*   Then we be applying mean normalization using the values of our previously generated mean and standard deviation\n","             \n","\n","\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"GlpDireFhAB3"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Set the height and width for resizing the images\n","img_height, img_width = 150, 150"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"NW9NRa1ippsU"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from (https://pytorch.org/vision/stable/transforms.html)\n","# Snippet from (https://github.com/mrdbourke/pytorch-deep-learning/blob/main/extras/solutions/04_pytorch_custom_datasets_exercise_solutions.ipynb)\n","# Create a sequence of image transformations using transforms.Compose\n","transform = transforms.Compose([\n","    # Resize the images to the specified height and width\n","    transforms.Resize((img_height, img_width)),\n","\n","    # Apply image augementation\n","    transforms.RandomHorizontalFlip(),        # Random horizontal flip to the images\n","    transforms.RandomRotation(20),            # Random rotation to the images within a range of 20 degrees\n","    transforms.RandomAffine(degrees=0, translate=(0.1, 0.1), scale=(0.9, 1.1), shear=0.1), # # Random affine transformation to the images\n","\n","    # Convert the images to PyTorch tensors\n","    transforms.ToTensor(),\n","\n","    # Normalize the tensor images using the calculated mean and standard deviation values for each channel\n","    transforms.Normalize(mean= dataset_stats[0], std= dataset_stats[1])\n","])"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"MvMrdNq9LDkR"},"source":["Let's view some random images from our image data comparing original version and the transformed version"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"9UUomFrupear"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from (https://github.com/mrdbourke/pytorch-deep-learning/blob/main/extras/solutions/04_pytorch_custom_datasets_exercise_solutions.ipynb)\n","def plot_transformed_images(data_dir, classes, transform, n=3, seed=35):\n","    \"\"\"Plots a series of random images from image_paths.\"\"\"\n","    random.seed(seed)\n","\n","    # Get a list of all image paths in the 15 classes\n","    image_paths = []\n","    for class_name in classes:\n","        class_dir = os.path.join(data_dir, class_name)\n","        for image_name in os.listdir(class_dir):\n","            image_paths.append(os.path.join(class_dir, image_name))\n","\n","    random_image_paths = random.sample(image_paths, k=n)\n","    for image_path_str in random_image_paths:\n","        image_path = Path(image_path_str)\n","        with Image.open(image_path) as f:\n","            fig, ax = plt.subplots(nrows=1, ncols=2)\n","            ax[0].imshow(f)\n","            ax[0].set_title(f\"Original \\nsize: {f.size}\")\n","            ax[0].axis(\"off\")\n","\n","            # Transform and plot image\n","            # permute() the image to make sure it's compatible with matplotlib\n","            transformed_image = transform(f).permute(1, 2, 0)\n","            ax[1].imshow(transformed_image)\n","            ax[1].set_title(f\"Transformed \\nsize: {transformed_image.shape}\")\n","            ax[1].axis(\"off\")\n","\n","            class_title = image_path.parts[-2]\n","\n","            fig.suptitle(f\"Class: {class_title}\", fontsize=16)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":1000},"id":"saQhJMfuJ03-","outputId":"42cdedd5-13f5-442d-e2a4-18bdeebebbee"},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":["WARNING:matplotlib.image:Clipping input data to the valid range for imshow with RGB data ([0..1] for floats or [0..255] for integers).\n","WARNING:matplotlib.image:Clipping input data to the valid range for imshow with RGB data ([0..1] for floats or [0..255] for integers).\n","WARNING:matplotlib.image:Clipping input data to the valid range for imshow with RGB data ([0..1] for floats or [0..255] for integers).\n"]},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["# Call the plot_transformed_images function with the list of image paths\n","plot_transformed_images(image_dir, classes, transform)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"355QmlAhppm8"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Set the path to  image directory\n","image_dir = \"/content/images\""]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"OtbLR6dS45-e"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Load the dataset applying the transform from above\n","dataset = ImageFolder(root=image_dir, transform=transform)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"kw4VVDWhpAUO"},"source":["<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">It is required for us to split our dataset into training and testing. This is used to monitor the model's performance on unseen data during training, which helps in detecting overfitting (when the model performs well on the training set but poorly on the validation set)."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"sGUBMedCpphu"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Split the dataset into training and validation subsets\n","train_size = int(0.8 * len(dataset))\n","val_size = len(dataset) - train_size\n","# Split the dataset randomly\n","train_ds, val_ds = random_split(dataset, [train_size, val_size])"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"sE_vAaGvppcy"},"outputs":[],"source":["batch_size = 8\n","# Create data loaders for training and validation subsets\n","train_dl = DataLoader(train_ds, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=True, num_workers=2)\n","val_dl = DataLoader(val_ds, batch_size=2*batch_size, shuffle=False, num_workers=2)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"OfFbxihav4Md"},"source":["<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">From the validation data loader it can be observed that we multiplied the batch size by 2. This to speed up the validation process without affecting the model's performance. During validation, the model doesn't update its weights, so there's no need to worry about the effect of a larger batch size on the weight updates. Instead, the model makes predictions on the validation dataset, and the primary concern is evaluating its performance. (Jason Brownlee, 2019)(https://machinelearningmastery.com/how-to-control-the-speed-and-stability-of-training-neural-networks-with-gradient-descent-batch-size/)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"GAeisaB_Post"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Why shuffle is true for train_ld and it is false for val_ld</H3>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> To avoid the model from learning any order or sequence in the data, machine learning practitioners frequently shuffle the training data. Instead of memorising the order in which the characteristics and labels occur, the model is driven to learn the patterns and correlations between them by randomly rearranging the data. In order to guarantee that the model is trained on random batches of data, it is crucial to use shuffle=True in the case of train_dl. This will stop the model from picking up any erroneous patterns or biases.</p>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> On the other hand, it is not required to shuffle validation data because it is used to assess the performance of the model following training. In order to ensure that the assessment is consistent with how the model would be evaluated in real-world circumstances, it is really preferable to preserve the data in the order in which it was initially gathered. To retain the order of the validation data, it is therefore appropriate to specify shuffle=False in the case of val_dl.</p>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"StwOGRynPost"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Why we set the num_workers to be 2</H3>\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">DataLoader is a PyTorch application that facilitates batch loading and iteration over data for effective machine learning model training. The number of subprocesses to employ for data loading is specified by the num_workers argument. It is usually set to 0, which indicates that the primary process will load the data.\n","\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">Multi-process data loading is possible when the num_workers option is set to a value higher than 0. This can speed up data loading, especially when processing data augmentations or importing big volumes of data. PyTorch produces extra subprocesses to load the data when num_workers is higher than 0, and each subprocess loads a batch of data concurrently.\n","\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">The num_workers argument in this instance is set to 2. To import the validation data, PyTorch will thus generate two more subprocesses. Especially if the data is stored on a slow storage source, such a hard drive, or if the data has to be preprocessed or enhanced before being supplied to the model, this can assist to speed up data loading.\n","\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">It's crucial to keep in mind that increasing num_workers too much might result in high CPU and memory utilisation, which can impede training. In light of the size of the dataset and the available computational resources, the workforce size should be carefully considered."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":688},"id":"TRYytn7juLQC","outputId":"4459ad6b-3931-45fe-977f-09078be17528"},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":["WARNING:matplotlib.image:Clipping input data to the valid range for imshow with RGB data ([0..1] for floats or [0..255] for integers).\n"]},{"data":{"text/plain":["(tensor(-2.7916), tensor(2.2076), tensor(1.1075), tensor(1.2300))"]},"execution_count":79,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 800x800 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["# Snippet from lectures\n","# Print out a sample of the transformed image\n","x, _ = next(iter(train_dl))\n","# Transpose is required to change from Channel x Height x Width to Height x Width x Channel (PyTorch is channel-first)\n","show_img(x[1].numpy().transpose(1,2,0));\n","x[0].min(), x[0].max(), x[0].mean(), x[0].std()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"RoxG_p3fwoWK"},"source":["## Preprocessing of Test Images.\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">We will need to preprocess our test images. When doing this it is important to we use the same mean and standard deviation values that were calculated for the training images and validation images and also resize the test images to be the same. This is because the model's weights have been adjusted based on the training data, so applying the same normalization and shape to the test images ensures that the model processes them in a consistent manner. If otherwise, it may introduce inconsistencies and make it difficult to interpret the model's performance on the test data. Also most resources on data preprocessing in machine learning are consistent in their transformations."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"-Hay-DJF3Y-_"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Set the height and width for resizing the test images (This is the same with the train and validation images)\n","img_height, img_width = 150, 150"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"Mw8tCHmMwn4V"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from (https://pytorch.org/vision/stable/transforms.html)\n","# Create a sequence of the  test images transformation using transforms.Compose\n","transform_test = transforms.Compose([\n","    # Resize the test images to the specified height and width\n","    transforms.Resize((img_height, img_width)),\n","\n","    # Convert to PyTorch tensors\n","    transforms.ToTensor(),\n","\n","    # Normalize the tensor images using the calculated mean and standard deviation values for each channel\n","    transforms.Normalize(mean= dataset_stats[0], std= dataset_stats[1])\n","])"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"XP6VYrQZ4x6H"},"outputs":[],"source":["test_images_dir = '/content/Test_images'"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"Fo7FXRGTwnzB"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Load the test dataset\n","test_ds = ImageFolder(root=test_images_dir, transform=transform_test)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"Xjfu_iAoAxN8","outputId":"44b86d22-3185-4a26-80ce-8a254a8e3909"},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["We have a total of 178 test images\n"]}],"source":["# Print the total number of test images\n","print(f\"We have a total of {len(test_ds)} test images\")"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"x3qoVuhhwnsN"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Create a DataLoader for the test dataset\n","batch_size = 8\n","test_dl = DataLoader(test_ds, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=False, num_workers=2)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"OsOl5XucxCn4"},"source":["# Build Deep Learning Model"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"XKojMVI8DZwk"},"source":[" ## Simple Convolution Neural Network (CNN)\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">A typical form of neural network used for image categorization, object recognition, and other computer vision applications is the convolutional neural network (CNN). In order to automatically learn features at various spatial scales, CNNs use a sequence of convolutional layers to take advantage of the 2D structure of the input data (such as photos).\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">The Simple CNN architecture shown above consists of four main components:\n","\n","<li>Convolutional layers: These layers perform convolutions on the input image, effectively scanning the image with a set of learnable filters. In this example, the first convolutional layer has 16 output channels, meaning it learns 16 different filters, each of which produces a different output feature map. The second convolutional layer has 32 output channels.</li>\n","\n","<li>Pooling layers: These layers reduce the spatial size of the input feature maps by downsampling them. In this example, the max pooling operation with kernel size 2 and stride 2 is used to downsample the feature maps by a factor of 2.</li>\n","\n","<li>Fully connected layers: These layers take the output of the last convolutional layer and flatten it into a 1D vector, which is then passed through one or more fully connected layers. In this example, the first fully connected layer has 256 output neurons.</li>\n","\n","<li>Activation function: The ReLU activation function is applied after each convolutional and fully connected layer to introduce non-linearity into the model.</li>\n","    \n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">The input picture is sent sequentially through each layer during the forward pass, and the output of the final layer is a collection of projected class probabilities. In order to reduce the discrepancy between the projected probabilities and the actual labels, the model is trained via backpropagation.\n","\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">In conclusion, this Simple CNN architecture provides a straightforward illustration of a CNN that can be applied to picture classification applications. However, with more difficult datasets, more complicated designs with deeper networks and more layers can achieve better accuracy."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"3WgAcKejQolI"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from lectures\n","class ConvRelu(nn.Module):\n","    def __init__(self, ch, cnt=2):\n","        super().__init__()\n","        layers = cnt * [nn.Conv2d(in_channels=ch, out_channels=ch, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1), nn.LeakyReLU(inplace=True)]\n","        self.block = nn.Sequential(*layers)\n","\n","    def forward(self, x):\n","        return self.block(x)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"Bq0dTXcKu4Vf"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from Lectures\n","net = nn.Sequential(\n","    nn.Conv2d(in_channels=3,  out_channels=16, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1), nn.LeakyReLU(),\n","     nn.Conv2d(in_channels=16, out_channels=32, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1), nn.LeakyReLU(),\n","    ConvRelu(32),\n","    nn.MaxPool2d(kernel_size=2, stride=2),\n","    nn.Conv2d(in_channels=32, out_channels=64, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1), nn.LeakyReLU(),\n","    ConvRelu(64),\n","    nn.MaxPool2d(kernel_size=2, stride=2),\n","    nn.AdaptiveAvgPool2d((4,4)),\n","    nn.Flatten(),\n","    nn.Linear(64*4*4,15)\n",").cuda() # Move the model to GPU"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"3LPSoTA8OHvk","outputId":"6aecefc6-267e-4cf3-c977-aa30c9d8eba8"},"outputs":[{"data":{"text/plain":["Sequential(\n","  (0): Conv2d(3, 16, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1))\n","  (1): LeakyReLU(negative_slope=0.01)\n","  (2): Conv2d(16, 32, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1))\n","  (3): LeakyReLU(negative_slope=0.01)\n","  (4): ConvRelu(\n","    (block): Sequential(\n","      (0): Conv2d(32, 32, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1))\n","      (1): LeakyReLU(negative_slope=0.01, inplace=True)\n","      (2): Conv2d(32, 32, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1))\n","      (3): LeakyReLU(negative_slope=0.01, inplace=True)\n","    )\n","  )\n","  (5): MaxPool2d(kernel_size=2, stride=2, padding=0, dilation=1, ceil_mode=False)\n","  (6): Conv2d(32, 64, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1))\n","  (7): LeakyReLU(negative_slope=0.01)\n","  (8): ConvRelu(\n","    (block): Sequential(\n","      (0): Conv2d(64, 64, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1))\n","      (1): LeakyReLU(negative_slope=0.01, inplace=True)\n","      (2): Conv2d(64, 64, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1))\n","      (3): LeakyReLU(negative_slope=0.01, inplace=True)\n","    )\n","  )\n","  (9): MaxPool2d(kernel_size=2, stride=2, padding=0, dilation=1, ceil_mode=False)\n","  (10): AdaptiveAvgPool2d(output_size=(4, 4))\n","  (11): Flatten(start_dim=1, end_dim=-1)\n","  (12): Linear(in_features=1024, out_features=15, bias=True)\n",")"]},"execution_count":266,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["# Print the summary of the model\n","net"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"D0z4DRpGhZc2","outputId":"7f38dc41-c0fb-4d24-8778-a6dc8b24f86b"},"outputs":[{"data":{"text/plain":["torch.Size([8, 3, 150, 150])"]},"execution_count":267,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["# Get the next batch of data  from the training data loader and print the shape\n","xb, _ = next(iter(train_dl))\n","xb.shape"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"JO_WOfbyh9U8","outputId":"39c3dbb8-f204-4baf-b42b-be6baf6f049a"},"outputs":[{"data":{"text/plain":["torch.Size([8, 15])"]},"execution_count":268,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["# Pass the simple CNN through input features and get the shape of the output tensor\n","net(xb.cuda()).shape"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"0fL8GhEqiww-"},"source":["## Training Loop"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"pSC4zPA4iy0k"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from Lectures\n","def one_epoch(net, loss, dl, opt=None, metric=None):\n","    # If an optimizer is provided, set the model to training mode else set to evaluation mode\n","    if opt: net.train()  # only affects some layers\n","    else:   net.eval()\n","\n","    L, M = [], []\n","    for xb, yb in tqdm(iter(dl), leave=False):\n","        xb, yb = xb.cuda(), yb.cuda()\n","        # If an optimizer is provided, perform a forward pass, compute the loss, and update the model weights\n","        if opt:\n","            y_ = net(xb)                      # Forward pass through the model\n","            l = loss(y_, yb)                  # Compute the loss using the provided loss function\n","            opt.zero_grad()                   # Zero the gradients before the backward pass\n","            l.backward()                      # Perform the backward pass to compute gradients\n","            opt.step()                        # Update the model weights using the optimizer\n","\n","        # Else if an optimizer is not provided, perform a forward pass and compute the loss without updating the model\n","        else:\n","            with tc.no_grad():                # Disable gradient calculation to save memory and computation\n","                y_ = net(xb)                  # Forward pass through the model\n","                l = loss(y_, yb)              # Compute the loss using the provided loss function\n","        L.append(l.detach().cpu().numpy())\n","        if metric: M.append(metric(y_, yb).cpu().numpy())\n","\n","    return L, M"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"y1kYfCGZi6-9"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from Lectures\n","# Define a lambda function to calculate the accuracy\n","accuracy = lambda y_,yb: (y_.max(dim=1)[1] == yb).float().mean()"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"QoGzi3IejAHi"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from Lectures\n","def fit(net, train_dl, val_dl, loss=nn.CrossEntropyLoss(), epochs=3, lr=3e-3, wd=1e-3, plot=True):\n","    # Create an Adam optimizer with the given learning rate  and weight decay\n","    opt = optim.Adam(net.parameters(), lr=lr, weight_decay=wd)\n","\n","    Ltr_hist, Lval_hist = [], []\n","    for epoch in trange(epochs):\n","        Ltr, _ = one_epoch(net, loss, train_dl,  opt)\n","        Lval, Aval = one_epoch(net, loss, val_dl, None, accuracy)\n","        Ltr_hist.append(np.mean(Ltr))\n","        Lval_hist.append(np.mean(Lval))\n","        print(f'epoch: {epoch}\\ttraining loss: {np.mean(Ltr):0.4f}\\tvalidation loss: {np.mean(Lval):0.4f}\\tvalidation accuracy: {np.mean(Aval):0.2f}')\n","\n","    # plot the losses\n","    if plot:\n","        _,ax = plt.subplots(1,1,figsize=(16,4))\n","        ax.plot(1+np.arange(len(Ltr_hist)),Ltr_hist)\n","        ax.plot(1+np.arange(len(Lval_hist)),Lval_hist)\n","        ax.grid('on')\n","        ax.set_xlim(left=1, right=len(Ltr_hist))\n","        ax.legend(['training loss', 'validation loss']);\n","\n","    return Ltr_hist, Lval_hist"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"KdH8BxicWjKw"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Snippet from Lectures\n","def count_params(md):\n","    cnt_all = 0\n","    ch, par = list(md.children()), list(md.parameters())\n","    for c in ch: cnt_all += count_params(c)\n","    if not ch and par:\n","        cnt_my = sum([p.numel() for p in par])\n","        print(md, cnt_my)\n","        return cnt_my\n","    return cnt_all"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"I-_Qyn9kWpZy","outputId":"82e923b7-52c2-480b-c3d1-429da0d97d31"},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Conv2d(3, 16, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1)) 448\n","Conv2d(16, 32, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1)) 4640\n","Conv2d(32, 32, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1)) 9248\n","Conv2d(32, 64, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1)) 18496\n","Conv2d(64, 64, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1)) 36928\n","Linear(in_features=1024, out_features=15, bias=True) 15375\n"]},{"data":{"text/plain":["85135"]},"execution_count":273,"metadata":{},"output_type":"execute_result"}],"source":["count_params(net)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":433,"referenced_widgets":["bf82ad89839a4539a8c1f66ec5819e98","7f02b1fecf6547bca91aa3544bb07483","db74d8a3460d497fb70c2d47c4c03108","f88026c3a4974b59877280a8bfe12e23","8970941348a84c1eb8c4181f2b0154b1","8e43fad1c48041749b051a2b80aa4460","a7284e5c3e92432d8367c6eec3a8305f","a00e7398a61f454397a05