s a total of 34 layers. Each residual block is made up of a shortcut connection that skips across convolutional layers and two or more or more convolutional layers. In order to avoid the issue of disappearing gradients that can arise in very deep networks, this shortcut link is made to allow gradients to travel directly through the network.\n","\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> The architecture of ResNet-34 is as follows:\n","\n","<li>Input layer: The input to the network is a 3x224x224 image.\n","\n","<li>Convolutional layer: The first layer is a convolutional layer with 64 filters of size 7x7 and a stride of 2, followed by a max pooling layer.\n","\n","<li>Residual blocks: There are four residual blocks in total, each consisting of multiple convolutional layers with 64 filters of size 3x3 and a shortcut connection that bypasses the convolutional layers. The number of convolutional layers in each block varies depending on the number of filters in the previous layer.\n","\n","<li>Fully connected layer: The output of the last residual block is flattened and passed through a fully connected layer with 1000 output neurons (corresponding to the 1000 classes in the ImageNet dataset).\n","\n","<li>Softmax activation: The output of the fully connected layer is passed through a softmax activation function to obtain the final class probabilities.\n","\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> It has been demonstrated that ResNet-34 performs at the cutting edge on a range of computer vision tasks, including segmentation, object identification, and picture classification. Even though ResNet-50 or ResNet-101 are more complex ResNet topologies, they nevertheless offer a fair balance between accuracy and processing economy."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"_mAEXiuCVs_y","outputId":"22f1d35f-fd8f-42e0-e5e7-d5247d6c746c"},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Conv2d(3, 64, kernel