"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["%%time\n","fit(resnet34.cuda(), train_dl, val_dl, epochs=10, lr=1e-5);"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"5QuujUWIXYbb"},"source":["## MobileNet\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> Convolutional neural network (CNN) architecture known as MobileNet was first presented in 2017 by Andrew G. Howard and colleagues at Google. The use of depthwise separable convolutions, a major invention of MobileNet, enables extremely effective computation and minimal memory utilisation on mobile and embedded systems.\n","\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">A depthwise separable convolution combines the depthwise and pointwise convolutional layers of a convolutional algorithm. Each input channel receives a single filter during depthwise convolution, producing a set of output channels with the same number of channels as the input. Pointwise convolution allows for linear combinations of the input channels and reduces the number of output channels to the required number by applying a 1x1 filter to the depthwise convolution's output.\n","\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> The architecture of MobileNet is as follows:\n","\n","<li>Input layer: The input to the network is a 3x224x224 image.\n","\n","<li>Convolutional layer: The first layer is a depthwise separable convolution with 32 filters of size 3x3 and a stride of 2.\n","\n","<li>Depthwise separable convolutional layers: There are 12 more depthwise separable convolutional layers, each with varying numbers of filters and strides. The final layer has 1024 filters.\n","\n","<li>Global average pooling: The output of the final convolutional layer is passed through a global average pooling layer, which computes the average value of each feature map across its spatial dimensions.\n","\n","<li>Fully connected layer: The output of the global average pooling layer is passed through a fully connected layer with a softmax activation function to obtain the final class probabilities.\n","\n","<p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">It has been demonstrated that MobileNet performs at the cutting edge on a range of computer vision tasks, including segmentation, object identification, and picture classification. It is particularly suited for embedded and mobile applications because it uses depthwise separable convolutions to obtain great accuracy at extremely minimal computational cost and memory utilisation."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"MfqvWeiNQamK","outputId":"f9a25a9e-53c0-4a3b-9e2e-c70b6d6bf976"},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Conv2d(3, 32, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(2, 2), padding=(1, 1), bias=False) 864\n","BatchNorm2d(32, eps=1e-05, momentum=0.1, affine=True, track_running_stats=True) 64\n","Conv2d(32, 32, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1), groups=32, bias=False) 288\n","BatchNorm2d(32, eps=1e-05, momentum=0.1, affine=True, track_running_stats=True) 64\n","Conv2d(32, 16, kernel_size=(1, 1), stride=(1, 1), bias=False) 512\n","BatchNorm2d(16, eps=1e-05, momentum=0.1, affine=True, track_running_stats=True) 32\n","Conv2d(16, 96, kernel_size=(1, 1), stride=(1, 1), bias=False) 1536\n","BatchNorm2d(96, eps=1e-05, momentum=0.1, affine=True, track_running_stats=True) 192\n","Conv2d(96, 96, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(2, 2), padding=(1, 1), groups=96, bias=False) 864\n","BatchNorm2d(96, eps=1e-05, momentum=0.1, affine=True, track_running_stats=True) 192\n","Conv2d(96, 24, kernel_size=(1, 1), stride=(1, 1), bias=False) 2304\n","BatchNorm2d(24, eps=1e-05, momentum=0.1, affine=True, track_running_stats=True) 48\n","Conv2d(24, 144, kernel_size=(1, 1), stride=(1, 1), bias=False) 3456\n","BatchNorm2d(144, eps=1e-05, momentum=0.1, affine=True, track_running_stats=True) 288\n","Conv2d(144, 144, kernel_size=(3, 3), stride=(1, 1), padding=(1, 1), groups=144, bias=False) 1296\n","BatchNorm2d(144, eps=1e-05, momentum=0.1, affine=True,