,"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x1000 with 6 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["# Visualize the selected images\n","rows, cols = 3, 2\n","fig, axes = plt.subplots(rows, cols, figsize=(10, 10))\n","\n","for i, index in enumerate(selected_indices):\n","    image = load_image(index)\n","    true_label = idx_to_class[test_truth[index].item()]\n","    pred_label = idx_to_class[test_preds[index].item()]\n","\n","    ax = axes[i // cols, i % cols]  # Determine the row and column of the current plot\n","    ax.imshow(image)\n","    ax.set_title(f\"True: {true_label}\\nPred: {pred_label}\")\n","    ax.axis(\"off\")\n","\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"85Q8WbPyhNt3"},"source":["# Observation and Conclusion"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"pM4WgXhTSBck"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> Observation and Conclusion </H3>\n","\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"> We may draw the conclusions and have the following conversation based on the reported accuracy:\n","\n","<li>The models with the highest reported accuracies of 0.7753 are loadedModel_Transfer_Learning and ResNet18_Transfer_Learning. This suggests that these models were successful in successfully learning the properties of the photos in the dataset and making reliable predictions.\n","\n","<li>The accuracy of the ResNet34_Transfer_Learning model is 0.6798, while the ResNet50_Transfer_Learning model is 0.6348. This implies that these models might not be as effective as the ResNet18 and loadedModel models for detecting the shelf life of fruits.\n","\n","<li>The model with the lowest reported accuracy, MobileNet_Transfer_Learning, gets a 0.5169 score. This could be as a result of the fact that MobileNet is a lightweight model that is prioritised for efficiency above accuracy. It might not be as proficient at picking up on the intricate details of the fruit photos as other models.\n","    \n","<li> It is essential to remember that the claimed accuracies could not represent a thorough assessment of the models. It is also important to take into account additional performance indicators like F1 score, recall, and accuracy.\n","\n","<li>The dataset that was used to develop and test the models may also have an effect on how well they work. The models may be overfit or have poor generalisation if the dataset is biassed or unrepresentative of real-world circumstances.\n","\n","<li>Finally, it is important to remember that transfer learning is an effective method that may considerably cut down on the time and training data needed to create a high-performing model. The pre-trained model used and the method used to optimise it can have a big influence on how well the final model performs.\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"-KXSwoZ17yfs"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">How the models can be improved...to get better accuracy</H3>\n","\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">There are numerous methods that may be utilised to increase the models' accuracy:\n","\n","<li>Expand the amount and variety of the training dataset: Giving the models more photos of fruits with a variety of colour, size, and texture changes can aid in the learning of more robust features and enhance accuracy.\n","\n","<li>Data augmentation: Adding rotation, scaling, and flipping operations to the already-existing pictures can assist to expand the training dataset and enhance the generalizability of the models.\n","\n","<li>Tuning the hyperparameters, which include the learning rate, batch size, and number of epochs, can enhance the performance of the models. It is possible to tune hyperparameters using methods like grid search or random search.\n","    \n","<li>Model architecture: To enhance the performance of the models, their architecture might be changed or improved. This might entail utilising alternative layer types, such as convolutional, pooling, or dropout layers, or increasing the depth or breadth of the layers.\n","\n","<li>Ensemble learning: When numerous models' predictions are combined, performance is frequently improved over that of any single model. To increase the models' accuracy, ensemble learning approaches like bagging, boosting, or stacking might be applied.\n","\n","<li>Transfer learning: Using a better pre-trained model, adjusting it differently, or applying transfer learning to a different\n","layer can all increase model accuracy."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"r-xwiB7V7IBr"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">How the project can be deployed to RasberryPi, IOT devices and mobile</H3>\n","\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">A new strategy is needed for deploying a fruit shelf-life detection model to Raspberry Pi, IoT, or mobile devices as opposed to a standard server or cloud platform. To deploy the project to these platforms, follow these steps:\n","\n","<li>Model optimisation: The model must first be made more effective and lightweight. This can be accomplished by deleting extra layers, shrinking the size of the input pictures, or shrinking the model size using methods like quantization or pruning.\n","\n","<li>The following step is to choose an appropriate framework for deployment. TensorFlow Lite, PyTorch Mobile, and ONNX are some well-liked frameworks for deployment on Raspberry Pi, IoT devices, or mobile devices.\n","    \n","<li>Platform-specific implementation: The project's implementation must be adapted to the particular platform being utilised. For image processing, for instance, deploying to Raspberry Pi or Internet of Things devices would entail utilising libraries like OpenCV, but deploying to mobile devices might need using native platform-specific frameworks like Core ML for iOS or ML Kit for Android.\n","\n","<li>Containerization: By bundling the programme and all of its dependencies into a single container, containerization is a technique that can streamline the deployment process. To deploy the project to several platforms, utilise the well-known containerization technology Docker.\n","\n","<li>Final step: The project must be integrated with the sensors and actuators in the hardware. This may entail interacting with the sensors and actuators on mobile devices via platform-specific APIs, or utilising GPIO pins on Raspberry Pi or IoT devices.\n","\n","Overall, careful evaluation of the platform-specific limits and needs is necessary before deploying the project to Raspberry Pi, IoT devices, or mobile devices. The project's deployment will be successful if the model is optimised, a suitable framework is chosen, platform-specific features are implemented, the programme is containerized, and hardware integration is done."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"Y9tdYPvD69op"},"source":["# Challenges faced when carrying out the project\n","\n","1. Handling variation in image size and resolutions.\n","2. Selecting an appropriate classification algorithm which will perform well on the dataset.\n","3. Difficulting in manually gathering images of those fruits in their different shelf lives.\n","4. Limited access to the number of gathered images\n","\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"omgLpe8vhTcJ"},"source":["#References"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"GretuaVPVnq2"},"source":["<H3 style=\"color:teal;\"><p style=\"font-family:Georgia;\">References</H3>\n","<ol style=\"font-family:Georgia;\"><li>Kang, H. and Chen, C., 2019. Fruit detection and segmentation for AppleHarvesting using visual sensor in orchards. Sensors (Basel, Switzerland) [online], 19 (20), 4599. Available from: http://dx.doi.org/10.3390/s19204599\n","    <li>Liu, X., Zhao, D., Jia, W., Ji, W., Ruan, C. and Sun, Y., 2019. Cucumber fruits detection in greenhouses based on instance segmentation. IEEE access: practical innovations, open solutions [on